[line]

[b]Vampires and werewolves[/b]
Two more sets that follow what you [i]are[/i] rather than the race you picked. They apply in human form, and they come on top of your race's set - so a Nord vampire gets both.

[b]Vampire[/b]
[list]
[*]Magicka Regeneration +50% [size=2](additive)[/size]
[*]Poison Resistance +50% [size=2](additive)[/size]
[*]Health Regeneration +50% below half health [size=2](additive)[/size]
[/list]

[b]Werewolf[/b]
[list]
[*]Maximum Stamina +50 [size=2](additive)[/size]
[*]Movement Speed +10% [size=2](additive)[/size]
[*]Attack Damage +20% below half health [size=2](multiplicative)[/size]
[/list]

Beast Form and Vampire Lord are left alone - vanilla already does plenty there, and these are meant for the human form. If you play a vampire, you also keep your race's passives now: becoming one swaps your race for the vampire version of it, which used to read as "not your race" and drop the set.

[line]

[b]The menu[/b]
Hit F1 and look for [b]Race Passives[/b]. One slider per set - the ten races, plus Vampire and Werewolf. 0-200%. The real numbers show up under each slider as you drag, so you can see what you're actually getting.

A few things worth knowing:
[list]
[*]100% is the default - that's the table above
[*]Additive effects scale straight up to 200%
[*]Multiplicative effects (shout cooldown, attack damage, prices, learning) stop at 100%. Past that they flip the other way, so I capped them there.
[*]0% switches that set off entirely
[/list]

[line]

[b]Settings carry over[/b]
Your settings go into [i]SKSE/Plugins/RacePassives.ini[/i] and get re-applied every time you load, so new characters and old saves share the same set. You can edit the file by hand too - hit "Reload config from ini" and it takes effect without restarting.

[line]

[b]Won't clash with your other mods[/b]
It only adds new records. Nothing vanilla gets overwritten, and nothing from another mod gets touched. Race overhauls like Imperious or Morningstar work fine next to it - their edits and these passives just coexist. Load order doesn't matter for conflicts either, since there's nothing to override.

[line]

[b]Installing[/b]
Install it like normal. The installer asks whether you want English or Chinese text.

[line]

[b]Uninstalling mid-save[/b]
The mod puts abilities on your character, so do this first:
[list=1]
[*]In game, F1 > [b]Overview[/b] > [b]Disable all (before uninstalling)[/b]
[*]Save
[*]Then remove the mod
[/list]
Skip that and the save usually still loads, but it'll keep references to records that no longer exist. [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/5031]ReSaver[/url] can clean those out.

